From 7e84d91b3460ac9d1bcd2ca0e2807b76bfd07e92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 13:16:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A refresh of a page's info leaves the info it was queued with
 as it was

After a page is saved, SiYuan queues a refresh of the page's info for the
page tree (refreshDocInfo0, as task.ReloadProtyle), and one for the page
above it. The refresh's handler wrote the page's sub-page count into the
docInfo map it was queued with. Since v3.8.7, the task queue's duplicate
check (containTask, from appendTaskWithDelayTimeout) compares a new task
with the running async tasks too, and areArgsEqual falls back to
reflect.DeepEqual, which reads a running refresh's map. When a refresh was
queued while another wrote its count, Go stopped the whole process with
"fatal error: concurrent map iteration and map write", and whatever was
talking to SiYuan got a cut connection (polka-siyuan's issue #49). v3.8.6
compared only the queued tasks and the running task that wasn't async, so
it never read a running refresh's map.

The handler now writes the count into a copy of docInfo (maps.Clone) and
pushes the copy, so a task's arguments don't change once it is queued.
The page tree gets the same info as before. The duplicate check doesn't
change, but it now sees a running refresh's info as it was queued: a
refresh of identical info queued while one runs is always dropped as its
duplicate, where before it was dropped only until the running one had
written its count. The window that adds is the push after the count.

The queue's other unique tasks with a map or slice argument only read it,
so they don't change: UpdateIDs' block-id map, which util.PushUpdateIDs
broadcasts after its caller has returned, and RefreshCrossTreeMoveRefs'
list, a copy the transaction makes for it, which refreshCrossTreeMoveRefs
reads.

The test, TestDocInfoRefreshLeavesTheInfoItWasQueuedWith in
doc_info_refresh_test.go, goes through refreshDocInfo0 and the real task
queue (task.ExecAsyncTaskJob). util.ReloadDocInfoGuard, which SiYuan calls
just before it pushes a page's info, holds the first push of a page last
changed years ago, so its humanized times read the same in each refresh;
the page is in a notebook of the test's own, so only its pushes count.
While the push is held, the same refresh is queued again, then the queue
runs what is due. On v3.8.7 with patches 0001 to 0009 it fails: the
running refresh's info has gained subFileCount, so the second refresh
isn't its duplicate and the page's info is pushed twice. With the change
it is pushed once.

Checks run on the series: the test fails without the change and passes
with it; SiYuan's whole kernel suite passes on the source, 35 packages,
with -count=1; the image builds, applying 0001 to 0010, and runs every
patch's tests. Two one-off checks: under the race detector, a 5 s burst
of identical refreshes while the queue ran them reported a race between
the handler's write and areArgsEqual's read on 0001 to 0009, and none
with the change; and on a throwaway of the image before and of the new
one, a page-tree session on SiYuan's websocket got the same reloadDocInfo
messages for a page given a sub-page, its subFileCount 0 and then 1.
---
 kernel/model/doc_info_refresh_test.go | 95 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 kernel/model/push_reload.go           |  7 +-
 2 files changed, 100 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 kernel/model/doc_info_refresh_test.go

